a new flaw has been found on Boeing 737  max Jets and it's expected to further  delay the plane's return to service the  737 max leet you'll recall was grounded  worldwide in March after two deadly  crashes overseas Kris Van Cleave is at  Reagan National Airport outside  Washington Chris it sounds like a  another setback for Boeing what's behind  it well good morning FAS pilots have  been going through all of the changes to  the 737 max in a flight simulator in  Seattle and it was during that testing  that they discovered this new issue  which they believe to be a pre-existing  problem with the airplane during what  was described as a failure scenario  where the nose of the plane was being  pushed down similar to what happened in  both max crashes they discovered a  microprocessor in the flight control  computer can become overloaded that  slowed down the test pilots ability to  respond to that nose down situation  essentially allowing it to continue  longer than they felt it should the FAA  is concerned here is if this scenario  presented itself during an actual flight  it could increase the chances of a crash  Boeing believes an additional software  update can address this newly-discovered  issue if it doesn't the company may have  to replace the component on all of the  grounded max planes yesterday Boeing CEO  Dennis Muhlenberg said by the time the  737 max is updated and certified it will  be one of the safest airplanes to ever  fly the FAA says it will not conduct the  airborne flight tests necessary to  approve the changes to the max until  this issue is resolved yesterday United  Airlines joined American and Southwest  in pulling the max off its flight  schedule until at least September 3rd  Anthony Kris Van Cleave with more  problems for the 737 max thanks Chris
 

